1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,700
So what I'm going to talk about to you today is something called Ethnography.

2
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:13,220
This is a type of research aimed at exploring the way human cultures work.

3
00:00:13,220 --> 00:00:16,673
It was first developed for use in anthropology,

4
00:00:16,673 --> 00:00:21,250
and it's also been used in sociology and communication studies.

5
00:00:21,250 --> 00:00:25,380
So what's it got to do with business, you may ask.

6
00:00:25,380 --> 00:00:34,517
Well, businesses are finding that ethnography can offer them deeper insight into the possible needs of customers, either present

7
00:00:34,517 --> 00:00:35,300
or future,

8
00:00:35,300 --> 00:00:40,810
as well as providing valuable information about their attitudes towards existing products.

9
00:00:40,810 --> 00:00:46,070
And ethnography can also help companies to design new products

10
00:00:46,070 --> 00:00:48,600
or services that customers really want.

11
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:55,090
Let's look at some examples of how ethnographic research works in business.

12
00:00:55,090 --> 00:01:01,070
One team of researchers did a project for a company manufacturing kitchen equipment.

13
00:01:01,070 --> 00:01:06,836
They watched how cooks used measuring cups to measure out things like sugar

14
00:01:06,836 --> 00:01:07,660
and flour.

15
00:01:07,660 --> 00:01:12,157
They saw that the cooks had to check and recheck the contents,

16
00:01:12,157 --> 00:01:17,538
because although the measuring cups had numbers inside them, the cooks couldn't see these

17
00:01:17,538 --> 00:01:17,960
easily.

18
00:01:17,960 --> 00:01:22,667
So a new design of cup was developed to overcome this problem,

19
00:01:22,667 --> 00:01:25,000
and it was a top seller.

20
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:31,655
Another team of ethnographic researchers looked at how cell phones were used in Uganda,

21
00:01:31,655 --> 00:01:32,520
in Africa.

22
00:01:32,520 --> 00:01:37,569
They found that people who didn't have their own phones could pay to use

23
00:01:37,569 --> 00:01:39,500
the phones of local entrepreneurs.

24
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:44,859
Because these customers paid in advance for their calls, they were eager to know

25
00:01:44,859 --> 00:01:47,750
how much time they'd spent on the call so far.

26
00:01:47,750 --> 00:01:53,880
So the phone company designed phones for use globally with this added feature.

27
00:01:53,880 --> 00:01:59,730
Ethnographic research has also been carried out in computer companies.

28
00:01:59,730 --> 00:02:05,440
In one company, IT systems administrators were observed for several weeks.

29
00:02:05,440 --> 00:02:10,903
It was found that a large amount of their work involved communicating with colleagues

30
00:02:10,903 --> 00:02:12,630
in order to solve problems,

31
00:02:12,630 --> 00:02:18,118
but that they didn't have a standard way of exchanging information from spreadsheets and

32
00:02:18,118 --> 00:02:18,460
so on.

33
00:02:18,460 --> 00:02:23,254
So the team came up with an idea for software that would help them

34
00:02:23,254 --> 00:02:24,080
to do this.

35
00:02:24,080 --> 00:02:30,347
In another piece of research, a team observed and talked to nurses working in

36
00:02:30,347 --> 00:02:30,970
hospitals.

37
00:02:30,970 --> 00:02:36,650
This led to the recognition that the nurses needed to access the computer records

38
00:02:36,650 --> 00:02:39,380
of their patients, no matter where they were.

39
00:02:39,380 --> 00:02:45,320
This led to the development of a portable computer tablet that allowed the nurses

40
00:02:45,320 --> 00:02:48,410
to check records in locations throughout the hospital.

41
00:02:48,410 --> 00:02:55,580
Occasionally, research can be done even in environments where the researchers can't be present.

42
00:02:55,580 --> 00:03:02,880
For example, in one project done for an airline, respondents used their smartphones to

43
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:08,536
record information during airline trips, in a study aiming at tracking the emotions of

44
00:03:08,536 --> 00:03:10,020
passengers during a flight.

45
00:03:10,020 --> 00:03:19,380
So what makes studies like these different from ordinary research?

46
00:03:19,380 --> 00:03:24,770
Let's look at some of the general principles behind ethnographic research in business.

47
00:03:24,770 --> 00:03:29,606
First of all, the researcher has to be completely open-minded - he

48
00:03:29,606 --> 00:03:35,004
or she hasn't thought up a hypothesis to be tested, as is the case

49
00:03:35,004 --> 00:03:36,810
in other types of research.

50
00:03:36,810 --> 00:03:42,080
Instead they wait for the participants in the research to inform them.

51
00:03:42,080 --> 00:03:46,464
As far as choosing the participants themselves is concerned,

52
00:03:46,464 --> 00:03:49,480
that's not really all that different from ordinary research

53
00:03:49,480 --> 00:03:54,085
- the criteria according to which the participants are chosen may be something as

54
00:03:54,085 --> 00:03:56,820
simple as the age bracket they fall into,

55
00:03:56,820 --> 00:04:00,292
or the researchers may select them according to their income,

56
00:04:00,292 --> 00:04:03,584
or they might try to find a set of people who all use a

57
00:04:03,584 --> 00:04:05,430
particular product, for example.

58
00:04:05,430 --> 00:04:11,480
But it's absolutely crucial to recruit the right people as participants.

59
00:04:11,480 --> 00:04:17,840
As well as the criteria I've mentioned, they have to be comfortable talking about themselves

60
00:04:17,840 --> 00:04:21,150
and being watched as they go about their activities.

61
00:04:21,150 --> 00:04:26,836
Actually, most researchers say that people open up pretty easily, maybe

62
00:04:26,836 --> 00:04:29,770
because they're often in their own home or workplace.

63
00:04:29,770 --> 00:04:34,781
So what makes this type of research special is that it's not just a

64
00:04:34,781 --> 00:04:40,492
matter of sending a questionnaire to the participants, instead the research is usually based

65
00:04:40,492 --> 00:04:44,080
on first-hand observation of what they are doing at the time.

66
00:04:44,080 --> 00:04:48,340
But that doesn't mean that the researcher never talks to the participants.

67
00:04:48,340 --> 00:04:55,945
However, unlike in traditional research, in this case it's the participant rather than the

68
00:04:55,945 --> 00:05:00,520
researchers who decides what direction the interview will follow.

69
00:05:00,520 --> 00:05:05,484
This means that there's less likelihood of the researcher imposing his

70
00:05:05,484 --> 00:05:08,780
or her own ideas on the participant.

71
00:05:08,780 --> 00:05:14,246
But after they've said goodbye to their participants and got back to their office,

72
00:05:14,246 --> 00:05:17,040
the researchers' work isn't finished.

73
00:05:17,040 --> 00:05:22,295
Most researchers estimate that 70 to 80 per cent of their time is spent

74
00:05:22,295 --> 00:05:28,213
not on the collecting of data but on its analysis - looking at photos, listening to recordings

75
00:05:28,213 --> 00:05:29,797
and transcribing them,

76
00:05:29,797 --> 00:05:30,700
and so on.

77
00:05:30,700 --> 00:05:35,260
The researchers may end up with hundreds of pages of notes.

78
00:05:35,260 --> 00:05:39,823
And to determine what's significant, they don't focus on the sensational things

79
00:05:39,823 --> 00:05:41,330
or the unusual things,

80
00:05:41,330 --> 00:05:46,857
instead they try to identify a pattern of some sort in all this data,

81
00:05:46,857 --> 00:05:49,630
and to discern the meaning behind it.

82
00:05:49,630 --> 00:05:56,632
This can result in some compelling insights that can in turn feed back to

83
00:05:56,632 --> 00:05:59,040
the whole design process.
